{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791314639,"nonce":"+HE8TPVH18+9tjnu7qfCHVOlcSg=","title":"HIR: simplify repeated scalar selects and tag expressions before native codegen","message":"The LLVM-off comparison on committed 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b shows Lua nbody kernel time around 172 ms on Cranelift versus 114 ms with LLVM. Both use the same interpreter-first policy; unsetting ZYLUA_LLVM disables optimizing-tier promotion. The full NUC comparison is /tmp/zyntax-nuc-no-llvm.log and /tmp/zyntax-nuc-no-llvm-*.json.\n\nThe generated advance HIR contains duplicate selects, idempotent integer tag ORs, and comparisons that decode a just-selected constant tag. CSE currently explicitly excludes Select, although it is a pure value operation. const_fold only forwards selects with a known boolean condition and known-operand boolean AND/OR. These are shared HIR opportunities before either native backend. Investigate bounded scalar simplifications with bit-exact behavior, including signed zero and NaNs, and preserve dominance/arm order. Do not change frontend rules or force tiering.\n\nDiagnostic HIR: /tmp/zyntax-cl-only-nbody-hir and /tmp/zyntax-llvm-nbody-hir. Local timings from these traced runs are not benchmark evidence. LLVM optimized advance removes many of these chains; Cranelift-only NUC measurements should establish whether the HIR changes materially help.","files":null}]}